WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.980
Every sense ends in the same currency: spikes that look alike.

2
00:00:07.880 --> 00:00:10.680
So how does a spike say what touched you, where, and how hard?

3
00:00:11.650 --> 00:00:17.910
In 1926, Edgar Adrian and Yngve Zotterman recorded one stretch receptor in a frog

4
00:00:17.910 --> 00:00:20.530
muscle. Then they hung weights on the muscle.

5
00:00:21.533 --> 00:00:24.193
A heavier weight stretches the receptor more.

6
00:00:24.893 --> 00:00:27.373
Will its spikes get bigger, or will they come more often?

7
00:00:33.367 --> 00:00:34.627
They come more often.

8
00:00:35.507 --> 00:00:37.187
Every spike stays the same size.

9
00:00:37.967 --> 00:00:40.507
And while a weight is held, the firing slowly falls.

10
00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:43.640
So the rate tells you how much.

11
00:00:46.750 --> 00:00:50.630
Now the what. Pressure on the closed eye is seen as light.

12
00:00:51.550 --> 00:00:54.910
Activity in the optic nerve means light, whatever started it.

13
00:00:55.470 --> 00:00:57.190
So the kind of sense comes from the line.

14
00:00:58.090 --> 00:01:00.530
Whether every quality works this way is still argued.

15
00:01:01.483 --> 00:01:06.163
A tuning curve plots a neuron's mean firing rate against one feature of the

16
00:01:06.163 --> 00:01:09.283
stimulus. The count varies from trial to trial.

17
00:01:10.163 --> 00:01:11.163
That spread is noise.

18
00:01:12.433 --> 00:01:15.813
Here is the one idea, for spikes counted over a fixed time.

19
00:01:16.673 --> 00:01:21.673
On each trial, a neuron's rate is its tuning curve, read at the stimulus, plus a

20
00:01:21.673 --> 00:01:23.453
fluctuation that averages to zero.

21
00:01:24.517 --> 00:01:29.157
Which neurons respond, and their relative firing rates, together tell you what,

22
00:01:29.417 --> 00:01:30.417
where and how much.

23
00:01:31.117 --> 00:01:35.397
A small change is easiest to see where the rate changes a lot, compared with the

24
00:01:35.397 --> 00:01:38.557
noise. Spike timing can carry information too.

25
00:01:39.550 --> 00:01:40.830
How is the rate set?

26
00:01:41.690 --> 00:01:45.330
In touch, force opens channels, mostly in the nerve ending itself.

27
00:01:46.250 --> 00:01:50.850
In a simple model, each open channel pulls the balance point toward 0 mV.

28
00:01:51.733 --> 00:01:53.913
So the receptor potential is graded.

29
00:01:54.833 --> 00:01:58.093
Each extra channel moves it less, as the voltage nears 0 mV.

30
00:02:01.583 --> 00:02:03.883
But a graded potential fades as it spreads.

31
00:02:04.763 --> 00:02:08.243
To reach the spinal cord, it must become spikes, which regenerate.

32
00:02:09.367 --> 00:02:11.327
Receptive fields help tell you where.

33
00:02:12.207 --> 00:02:17.087
Many afferents used for fine touch have small fields on a fingertip and larger ones

34
00:02:17.087 --> 00:02:20.887
elsewhere. Field size also depends on receptor type.

35
00:02:21.850 --> 00:02:23.590
Endings are tuned in time, too.

36
00:02:24.710 --> 00:02:26.550
Rapidly adapting endings report change.

37
00:02:27.590 --> 00:02:31.350
Slowly adapting endings keep firing, and report the stimulus itself.

38
00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:34.740
So a spike means what its neuron is tuned to.

39
00:02:35.660 --> 00:02:36.740
Next, neighbours compare.
